      "end": 53.7,
      "text": "These two Python lines look alike, but they run very differently. Square brackets build a list. Python loops now, calculates every square, and stores every result in memory. Parentheses build a generator expression. Python returns a generator object before calculating the results. When next, or a for loop, asks for a value, Python resumes the generator, calculates one item, yields it, then pauses with its state saved. Ask again, and it continues. Lists support indexing, length, reuse, and repeated passes. Generators produce values once as you iterate, so an exhausted generator stays exhausted. Use a list when you need the whole collection or random access. Use a generator for large files, database rows, model batches, or streaming pipelines where holding everything wastes memory. Same loop idea; different runtime. Follow for more Python foundations for AI engineering, save this comparison, and use it in your next project.",
